{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_4e9de9df5c15832ed4676ec1","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"minimax-m3.1-flash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fixed. Two separate defects, one of which masked the other.\n\n**1. The billing bug (the actual total error)** — `billing/currency.py:7`\n\n```python\nreturn round(amount / nok_per_unit(currency), 2)   # was\nreturn round(amount * nok_per_unit(currency), 2)   # now\n```\n\n`NOK_PER_UNIT` in `billing/rates.py:4` is documented as *NOK per one unit of the foreign currency* — a multiplier. The code divided by it, so 10 EUR came out as 10/11.50 = 0.87 NOK instead of 115 NOK. The NOK-only case passed because NOK's rate is 1.0, and division by 1.0 hides the error. Fixed at the conversion, not in the report.\n\n**2. A syntax error in the test files** — `tests/test_report.py:2` and `tests/test_invoice.py:2`\n\nBoth had `sys.path.insert(...)from billing.invoice import ...` on one line (missing newline), so `python tests/test_report.py` died with a `SyntaxError` before running a single assertion. The mixed-currency failure was never actually being reported. Split the lines.\n\nTest results after the fix:\n\n```\nok   test_mixed_currency_report\nok   test_nok_only_report\n```\n\n```\nok   test_nok_line\n```\n\nOne note on the rate table, not a defect I touched: `SEK: 1.00` looks like a placeholder rather than a real rate, and `NOK: 1.00` is the only reason the NOK-only test passed pre-fix. If SEK lines exist in production invoices, that rate needs verifying against the bank feed."}],"usage":{"input_tokens":23680,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"60bc1fb0-ea8e-4be5-ba5b-5035250e66dc","uuid":"42fb80b1-e5cd-4dfd-af83-8f643e97ff03","timestamp":"2026-10-30T13:08:03.200Z"}
